<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shilluk people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chollo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shilluk, or Chollo, are a Nilotic people living mainly along the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, around Malakal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fashoda">Kodok</a>. Their kingdom, founded according to tradition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyikang">Nyikang</a>, was ruled by a sacred king, the reth, from Fashoda. Farmers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> keepers and fishermen, they raided down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in canoe fleets in the 17th and 18th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shilluk-people.jpg" alt="Shilluk people" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matson Photo Service, photographer. (G. Eric and Edith Matson Photograph Collection) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sudan_Malakal_two_Shiluki_1936.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Shilluk, who call themselves Chollo, are a major Luo Nilotic people living on the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> in the Upper Nile state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. They speak Dhøg Cøllø, which belongs to the Luo branch of the Western Nilotic subfamily, and they are the third-largest ethnic group of South Sudan after the Dinka and the Nuer. According to one account they formed their kingdom in 1454. Its king, the reth, came from the divine lineage of the culture hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyikang">Nyikang</a>, who is believed to affect the nation's health.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The kingdom was historically a patriarchal monarchy, and society was somewhat hierarchical, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of royals, nobles, commoners and enslaved people. Today the Shilluk are governed by an elected headman chosen by a council of hamlet heads. Before the Second Sudanese Civil War the Shilluk also lived on the northern bank of the Sobat River, near where it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><p>Christian missions divided them: the Episcopal Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> dates its beginning to the late 19th century, when the Church Mission Society sent missionaries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> ran stations at Lul, Detwoc, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> and Yoynyang on the western bank, and the American Inland Mission ran one at Doleib Hill. The Shilluk were a minority in the Sudan People's Liberation Movement for most of the Second Sudanese Civil War.</p><p>In the summer of 2010 the Sudan People's Liberation Army, trying to disarm the people and stop a local Shilluk rebellion, burned several villages in the Shilluk Kingdom and killed an untold number of civilians. More than 10,000 people were displaced during the rainy season and fled into the forest, often without bedding, shelter or food, and many children died from hunger and cold. Violence began again in April 2011 with a crackdown on rebel-controlled regions, and broke out again in late 2022.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most Shilluk are settled farmers, and both sexes do agricultural work. As among most Nilotic peoples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-raising is a large part of the economy, though agriculture and fishing matter more than usual. Marriage involves bride-wealth, which traditionally included about ten head of cattle, several sheep and symbolic spears, although the amount depended on the groom's means. The ceremonies include a cattle exchange, a wedding feast at the bride's home and a symbolic mock battle between the families. Most Shilluk have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, some follow the traditional religion or a mixture of the two, and a few have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shilluk people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…uncertain: they appear in the sources as a cattle-keeping people of the southern Gezira and the upper Blue Nile, and theories linking them to the <b>Shilluk</b>, to Bornu or to Umayyad refugees from Arabia remain unproven, the last being a later genealogy that supported the dynasty’s Muslim credentials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to <b>Shilluk</b> tradition, the kingdom was founded by Nyikang, who led his people north along the White Nile and settled them on its west bank.</blockquote><a href="/state/shilluk-kingdom">Shilluk Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
